window.docContents[116].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-6":{"contents":"\n\nA catalogued kind pairs a dimensioned quantity-kind with its\nexact source in the series — the part, the printed item designation (e.g. \"6-51.1\"), the\nprincipal quantity symbol, and the coherent SI unit symbol. The citation travels with the\nkind as data, so the source of each definition can be rendered or audited downstream\nrather than kept in a comment.\n\nRealized as structure CataloguedKind over a DimensionedKind, with cite rendering\ne.g. IEC 80000-6, Edition 2.0, 2022-11 item 6-51.1 (the one IEC-published part of the\ncatalogue). The catalogue lists all 85 items in item order — electric current (6-1),\nelectric charge (6-2.1), magnetic flux (6-22.1), the impedance family (6-51.1 … 6-51.5),\nthe AC power quantities (6-56 … 6-61), active energy (6-62), … — each paired with its\ncoherent SI unit symbol.\n\nA capacitance carries the charge generator squared (item 6-13). The catalogued\ncapacitance kind has charge-exponent two in PhysLib's dimension group — the farad's\nC²·M⁻¹·L⁻²·T² reproduced as a checked computation over the new electric-current base\naxis, not an annotation. Uses the dimension map.\n\nProved as capacitance_dim_charge : capacitance.dim.charge = 2 in the Iso80000\nlibrary, discharged by norm_num over the PhysLib dimension lemmas for the charge\ngenerator. The companions electricCurrent_dim_charge = 1 and electricCurrent_dim_time\n= -1 (item 6-1, the ampere as C·T⁻¹), resistance_dim_charge = -2 (item 6-46, the\nohm), and magneticFlux_dim_charge = -1 (item 6-22.1, the weber) record the rest of the\nelectromagnetic algebra.\n\nAn ampere and a volt are not commensurable. Electric current and electric potential are\ndistinct kinds, so their units cannot be compared by ratio — a type-level fact, not a\nruntime check. Uses metrological units.\n\nProved as ampere_voltPotential_not_commensurable : ¬ ampere.Commensurable voltPotential\nby unfolding Commensurable to the underlying kinds and decide. The ampere and the ohm\nare each WellFormed (electric current and resistance are ratio-scale).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-6 — Electromagnetism","header":"25.1. Quantity-kinds and units of IEC 80000-6","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-6-___-Electromagnetism--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-6"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Scale-types-and-the-operations-they-license":{"contents":"* This work* Dybkær (2009)* ISO 80000-1:2022* VIM 3 (JCGM 200:2012)* VIM 4 2CD* ScaleType* Ch. 12, Fig. 12.21* 4.1 (no term)* —* 1.1 Note 3 (no term)* ScaleType.AllowsOrder* §12.16* 4.1 (comparable by order)* 1.26 ordinal quantity* 1.33 ordinal quantity* ScaleType.AllowsDifference* §12.19* —* —* 1.4 interval quantity* ScaleType.AllowsRatio* §12.20* 4.1 (comparable by ratio)* —* 1.3 ratio quantity* ScaleType.HasMagnitude* §13.3.1* 4.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* ScaleType.allows_mono* Ch. 12 (no section given)* —* —* 1.3 Note 2, 1.4 Note 2 (no term)\n\n* Agreement. All four sources separate properties by the comparisons that apply to\nthem. HasMagnitude draws the line where VIM 3 draws it: a quantity\nhas a magnitude and a nominal property has none. AllowsOrder begins at the ordinal\nlevel, as the ordinal quantity of both VIM editions does.* VIM 3 names two levels, not four. It defines the ordinal quantity and the nominal\nproperty and leaves the interval and ratio levels without a term: every quantity that\nis not ordinal is treated as entering quantity calculus. AllowsDifference has no\ncounterpart there.* ISO 80000-1:2022 describes three comparisons and covers one. Clause 4.1 says that\nproperties make things comparable by equivalence, some also by order, and some also by\nratio. It does not mention comparison by difference, and its scope leaves ordinal\nquantities and nominal properties out. The quantities it treats are the ratio-scale\nones.* VIM 4 2CD names all four, and defines quantity itself by them (comparability by\nratio, by difference, or by order). It gives no term for the classification as a whole,\nwhich is what ScaleType is; the four levels are listed in a note.* Departure in terms. Dybkær and this work say differential and rational for the\nkinds, and interval and ratio for the scale types; VIM 4 2CD says interval\nquantity and ratio quantity.* Departure in content. allows_mono is a theorem: an operation licensed at one scale\ntype is licensed at every richer one. The VIM 4 2CD notes state the two instances that\nmatter (a ratio quantity is ordered and has differences; an interval quantity is\nordered) and no source states the general law.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Standards Reference — PKC beside ISO 80000-1 and the VIM","header":"34.2. Scale types and the operations they license","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Scale-types-and-the-operations-they-license"}});